<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">L'Escalade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1602">1602</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">L'Escalade was a surprise night attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 11–12 December 1602 by troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-i-duke-of-savoy">Charles Emmanuel I, Duke of Savoy</a>, who tried to scale the city walls with ladders. The citizens beat off the assault, and the Treaty of Saint-Julien of 1603 recognised the city's independence from Savoy. The victory is still celebrated every December in Geneva.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/l-escalade.jpg" alt="L'Escalade" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geneva Urbs, vers 1603, Matthias Quad (1557 - après 1608) ou atelier de Franz Hogenberg (Cologne, XVIe - début XVlle siècle) … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escalade-battle-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Emmanuel I seized the Marquisate of Saluzzo in 1588 and kept it by the Treaty of Lyon in 1601, ceding Bresse and Bugey to France, but his night assault on Geneva, the <b>Escalade</b> of 1602, failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1602 Genevans repelled Savoy's night attack, the <b>Escalade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Republic of Geneva</a></li></ul></section>
